  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 245 AM CST Wed Nov 18 2026\n\nSouth-southeasterly winds have remained steady into early this \nmorning with much of our MN counties gusting to 20-30 MPH. Winds \nwill only increase into late this morning as a strong, but fast mid-\nlevel trough shoots northeast through the Northern Plains. With our \nlack of moisture and the synoptic lift mostly off to our northwest, \nwe will not see any precip with this trough. Instead, we'll receive \na strong increase in southerly winds as a 45-50 knot LLJ moves \nthrough MN and WI today. Surface winds are expected to maximize from \nmid-morning to early afternoon, particularly over western and \nsouthern MN where sustained values will reach 20-25 MPH and gusts \nreach 35-45 MPH. Areas along I-90 and the Buffalo Ridge may flirt \nwith Wind Advisory criteria but thinking gusts and their duration \nwill fall just short of needing a headline. Winds taper off a bit to \nthe north and west but will still be breezy with gusts reaching 25-\n35 MPH. Winds will slightly slow this afternoon and evening as \nclouds begin to clear. Temperatures will be warm again today with \nhighs for most of the CWA in the upper 50s to near 60. Low to even \nmid 60s are expected along the Buffalo Ridge. We'll cool into the \nlow to mid 40s tonight with winds turning more westerly by Thursday \nmorning as the trough exits east. Highs Thursday will be few degrees \ncooler (50s) and winds will still be pretty strong over west-central \nMN (gusts nearing 30 MPH). Winds calm significantly during Thursday \nnight while lows fall into the mid to upper 30s.\n\nFor Friday, southerly winds will increase again as a LLJ moves into \nthe Central Plains. This LLJ will develop in response to an upper-\nlevel trough in the western CONUS. So expect another windy day with \nstrongest winds expected in southern MN where gusts could reach 35 \nMPH. Strong WAA is also expected leading to mid 50s to potentially \nmid 60s for highs on Friday. It certainly looks like Friday will be \nthe warmest day we see until next Spring. The aforementioned upper-\nlevel trough is forecast to eject east of the Rockies during \nSaturday. However, guidance has trended towards a more positive tilt \nin the trough axis and a more disorganized arrival of synoptic \nenergy over the Upper Midwest. PoPs (in the form of rain) first \nappear back in the forecast during later Friday, mainly over \nnorthern half of MN, as surface cyclogenesis occurs in eastern \nND/northwestern MN. As the surface low deepens and travels northeast \nduring Saturday, it will drag a cold front west to east through \nMN/WI. Our best chance for widespread rainfall continues to be \nSaturday as rain is forecast to develop along the front. Though, \nrainfall amounts have trended downward with the maxima slightly \nfarther east in southeastern MN and WI. We'll continue to monitor \nmodel trends for Saturday. We should see rain, but how much and \nwidespread is in question with lower rainfall amounts seeming more \nlikely compared to earlier forecasts.\n\nMuch cooler air will follow the front Saturday night and last into \nat least the first half of next week. Temperatures will be much \ncloser to normal with overnight lows well below freezing and daytime \nhighs struggling to exceed the upper 30s. A trailing mid-level \ndisturbance could bring some light snow to northern MN Saturday \nthrough Sunday, though this precipitation is currently favored to be \nnorth of our CWA. After Sunday, long-range guidance shows the split \nupper-level flow developing over the central CONUS. Individual waves \nwithin this pattern vary between models, but overall no\nsignificant systems look to be on the horizon for the first \nhalf of next week. We'll have wait and see if our precipitation \nfortunes change as we get towards the latter half of next week.",
